From 2438b7562a6e342cdea8c5fab52f75bf30d4aa6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 13:18:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Android: GBA link diagnostics and manual screen switch

Socket 2 links to XD but socket 3 never does, and the Android bottom
screen auto-picks one GBA with no way to look at the other, so there is
currently no way to tell whether socket 3 is even resetting or whether
its joybus window ever opens. Add both halves of that ground truth.

Diagnostics: SI_DeviceGBAEmu publishes a per-SI-channel snapshot of the
link state -- link open now, link-window open count, auto-resets issued,
established/battle-locked latches, last joybus command byte, and how
many polls XD spent probing this port. It is written from the existing
poll and is strictly a tap: every change is an added counter or store,
no threshold, condition or branch moved, so link and reset behaviour is
bit-for-bit what it was. Reset counting covers all three paths that
actually issue one (solo RequestReset, netplay Pad::SetGBAReset, and the
X-button handler). The snapshot is atomic because the UI thread reads it
while the emulation thread writes it.

GbaHostBridge.getLinkDiag() hands one port's snapshot to Kotlin packed
into a long -- allocation-free, since GbaScreenView repaints it every
frame -- and the on-screen readout gains "lk/win/rst/prb/est/lck/cmd"
per port. The diagnostic text now shrinks to fit the view instead of
running off the edge, and its Paint/StringBuilder are hoisted out of
the draw path.

Screen switch: a long press on the GBA view cycles the bottom screen to
the next GBA. It only arms on bare canvas, never on a touch button, so
holding A for half a second still just holds A; a tap is byte-for-byte
the old path. The cycled choice sticks until the core behind it goes
away, then falls back to the automatic pick.

The switch is local-GBAs-only, so it cannot be used to peek at an
opponent's screen in netplay. Seeing the other player's handheld would
expose their party and their move selection live, so it is blocked
twice: a device is only cyclable if its CoreInfo.isLocal is true (that
flag comes from NetPlay::GetPadDetails().is_local, and in a netplay
battle exactly one link device is local), and the switch refuses
outright while any netplay session exists. A manual pick is also
re-validated on every core update and dropped the moment it stops being
local or present. Input focus follows the same rule and is never
requested for a GBA this player does not own.
